.TH MINSEC-FILTER.TOML 5 "2026-08-24" "minsec" "File Formats"
.SH NAME
minsec-filter.toml \- minsec log-source and matching-filter definition format
.SH SYNOPSIS
.I /etc/minsec/filters/name.toml
.SH DESCRIPTION
A filter definition selects log sources and describes lines that count as
failures for
.BR minsec (1).
Definitions are TOML files with top-level keys.
Unknown keys are errors.
.PP
Custom files are discovered by filename stem.
A custom
.I filters/name.toml
completely replaces the embedded filter named
.IR name ;
filter definitions are not merged.
The
.B [filters.name]
table in
.BR minsec.toml (5)
can then enable the filter and replace its files, journal selector, ports, or
retry policy.
.SH KEYS
.TP
.BI name " = string"
Filter name recorded in status and event records.
This key is required.
For predictable lookup and reporting, it should match the filename stem and
contain only lowercase letters, digits,
.BR . ,
.BR _ ,
and
.BR \- .
.TP
.BI description " = string"
Short description displayed by
.BR "minsec filters" .
Default: empty.
.TP
.BI files " = [path-pattern, ...]"
Log files to follow when this filter does not use the journal.
Shell-style glob patterns are accepted.
Existing files are normally opened at end of file; missing paths are watched
for creation.
Default: empty.
.TP
.BI journal " = { units = [...], identifiers = [...], comm = [...] }"
Journal selectors.
.B units
match
.BR _SYSTEMD_UNIT ,
.B identifiers
match
.BR SYSLOG_IDENTIFIER ,
and
.B comm
matches
.BR _COMM .
Every array entry is an alternative: a record matching any configured entry is
routed to the filter.
All arrays default to empty.
.TP
.BI prefilter " = [literal, ...]"
Case-sensitive literal substrings used as a cheap check before regular
expression matching.
A file line proceeds if any literal is present.
Prefilters are skipped for journal records, whose metadata already routes them
and whose message omits a traditional syslog prefix.
Default: empty, which disables the check.
.TP
.BI patterns " = [regex, ...]"
Required list of regular expressions describing failure lines.
Each expression must contain an address token such as
.BR <HOST> .
Patterns are tried as one ordered alternation; the first matching pattern with
a parseable address supplies the result and its zero-based pattern number.
.TP
.BI ignore " = [regex, ...]"
Regular expressions for lines that must not count even when a main pattern
matches.
The same token expansion applies, so
.B <HOST>
may be used here as well.
Default: empty.
.TP
.BI ports " = [integer, ...]"
Service-port metadata carried into effective policy and inspection output.
Default: empty.
The current nft backend drops banned sources on all ports, so this list does
not restrict its rules.
.SH PATTERN LANGUAGE
Patterns use the Rust
.B regex
syntax.
They are case-sensitive unless the expression changes flags and are searched
anywhere in the line unless explicitly anchored with
.B ^
or
.BR $ .
Look-around and backreferences are not supported.
.PP
TOML literal strings in single quotes are convenient for patterns because a
backslash is preserved without TOML escaping:
.PP
.nf
patterns = ['failed from <HOST> port \ed+']
.fi
.PP
The following fail2ban-style tokens are expanded before compilation:
.TP
.BR <HOST> ", " <ADDR> ", " <IP>
Capture an IPv4 or IPv6 address.
.TP
.B <IP4>
Capture an IPv4 address only.
.TP
.B <IP6>
Capture an IPv6 address only.
.TP
.BI <F- NAME >...< /F-NAME >
Create a named capture; the name is lowercased.
.B <F-USER>...</F-USER>
is returned as the optional username by the matcher.
Other named captures may be used for expression structure but are not otherwise
consumed by the current engine.
.PP
The Perl-style classes
.BR \ed ,
.BR \eD ,
.BR \es ,
.BR \eS ,
.BR \ew ,
and
.B \eW
are rewritten to ASCII-only classes.
Captured addresses are parsed after matching, so syntactically matching but
invalid addresses such as an IPv4 octet over 255 are rejected.
.SH SOURCE BEHAVIOR
For each enabled filter, minsec chooses the journal when all of these are true:
global journal use is enabled, journal support is available, and the definition
has at least one journal selector.
Otherwise minsec follows the filter's files.
It does not consume both source types for the same filter.
.PP
Journal unit metadata is trusted directly.
Identifier and command selectors are honored only for records whose sender UID
is a system account (UID 0 through 999), preventing unprivileged use of
.BR logger (1)
from spoofing service messages.
.SH BUILT-IN FILTERS
The binary embeds these definitions:
.PP
.nf
apache-auth    dovecot       nginx-auth     postfix
postfix-sasl   proftpd       roundcube      sshd
webmin         wordpress
.fi
.PP
Use
.B minsec filters
for descriptions and the installed binary's authoritative list.
.SH EXAMPLE
.nf
name = "myapp"
description = "My application login failures"
files = ["/var/log/myapp.log"]
journal = { identifiers = ["myapp"] }
prefilter = ["login failed"]
ports = [8443]
patterns = [
  'login failed for <F-USER>\eS+</F-USER> from <HOST>',
]
ignore = [
  'health-check account',
]
.fi
.PP
Enable and test it with:
.PP
.nf
minsec enable myapp
minsec check --all
minsec test myapp /var/log/myapp.log
.fi
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/minsec/filters/*.toml
Custom definitions and built-in replacements.
.TP
.I /etc/minsec/minsec.toml
Policy and enablement configuration.
.TP
.I /etc/minsec/conf.d/*.toml
Policy and enablement drop-ins.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR minsec (1),
.BR minsec.toml (5),
.BR regex (7)
